% introduction.tex — expanded for 8-page target

\section{Introduction}

Multi-turn jailbreak attacks distribute adversarial intent across individually benign turns. Crescendo~\citep{russinovich2024crescendo}, Foot-in-the-Door~\citep{fitd2024}, and Deceptive Delight~\citep{deceptivedelight2024} all rely on persuasion as their core mechanism: \citet{zeng2024pap} catalogue 40 persuasion techniques across jailbreak families, and \citet{hagendorff2026reasoning} identify strategies in 97\% of successful attacks. Because individual attack turns occupy the same embedding region as benign text~\citep{bullwinkel2025repeng}, no single-turn filter can distinguish them from legitimate conversation.

Existing defenses leave a critical gap. Production classifiers such as Llama Guard~\citep{inan2023llamaguard}, WildGuard~\citep{han2024wildguard}, and ShieldGemma~\citep{zeng2024shieldgemma} operate on individual turns and therefore miss manipulation that unfolds across exchanges. Multi-turn defenses have begun to model conversation-level structure: DeepContext~\citep{deepcontext2026} pairs fine-tuned BERT with a multi-layer GRU, BIID~\citep{tong2025biid} infers turn-level intentions, and G-Guard~\citep{gguard2025} applies graph neural networks. These systems achieve strong in-distribution performance, but each is evaluated on a single attack family. None answers the central question: \emph{what representation properties enable a detector to generalize across attack families it has never seen?}

Pretrained language understanding does not suffice at base model scale: vanilla DeBERTa-v3~\citep{he2021deberta} with temporal aggregation (cf.\ DeepContext) drops from F1\,=\,1.000 in-distribution to 0.312 on OOD Deceptive Delight, and GPT-5.5 reaches only 0.191--0.449 F1 with 30--47\% unparseable responses (Appendix~\ref{sec:llm_judge}). The bottleneck is distributional: cross-attack generalization requires domain adaptation, not a better classifier.

Our pipeline, \textbf{DisPeD} (Disentangled Persuasion Detection), separates encoder adaptation (Stage~1) from temporal classification (Stage~2, a frozen BiGRU; Figure~\ref{fig:architecture}). Seven Stage~1 variants each isolate a single factor while holding all others constant (Section~\ref{sec:method}); the contribution is the decomposition, not the pipeline.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig1_architecture.pdf}
\caption{DisPeD architecture. Stage~1 adapts a DeBERTa encoder on jailbreak conversation text (seven variant objectives). Stage~2 aggregates frozen \texttt{[CLS]} embeddings via BiGRU for conversation-level jailbreak detection. Only user turns are processed.}
\label{fig:architecture}
\end{figure}

Controlled ablation across DeBERTa-v3 and RoBERTa~\citep{liu2019roberta} on four attack families and two training compositions shows that for base-size encoders, classification-based adaptation is the most robust variant on synthetic multi-turn OOD attacks. MLM collapses on mixed-LLM training (F1\,=\,0.289 $\approx$ vanilla) while classification DAPT maintains 0.818; RoBERTa-large (355M) handles ActorAttack~\citep{ren2024actorattack} without adaptation. Classification supervision is associated with substantially lower prediction variance on synthetic data but not on human-authored conversations (Section~\ref{sec:human_data}).

Our contributions:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*, itemsep=2pt, topsep=4pt]
  \item \textbf{Systematic decomposition identifying the most robust adaptation and three counter-intuitive findings.} Controlled ablation across thirteen encoder configurations on synthetic multi-turn data shows classification-based DAPT is the most robust variant; unsupervised pretraining is effective on single-source data but collapses on mixed-LLM training. We report three supplementary findings that challenge common assumptions: scrambled labels perform comparably to correct labels (descriptive evidence at $n$=3 seeds), temporal order is irrelevant ($\Delta < 1$\,pp), and label granularity does not affect detection.

  \item \textbf{Architecture- and scale-dependent observation on domain adaptation.} Three encoder scales (125M, 184M, 355M) suggest adaptation need shrinks with model size, though encoders differ in architecture; same-architecture scaling is needed to isolate scale. RoBERTa-large handles structurally complex attacks without adaptation (Section~\ref{sec:cross_encoder}).

  \item \textbf{Condition-dependent adaptation effects.} Classification supervision is associated with substantially lower prediction variance on synthetic data (3 seeds), but this benefit reverses on human-authored data. Mixed-LLM training validates that classification-based DAPT remains the strongest variant across training compositions (Section~\ref{sec:discussion}). The 9-class taxonomy serves as interpretability tool and ablation infrastructure (Section~\ref{sec:strategy}). Continued pretraining is 266$\times$ faster than LLM-as-judge and near-saturates at $N$=50 (AA\,=\,0.969; Appendix~\ref{app:data_efficiency}).
\end{itemize}

% related_work.tex — D048 deep writing optimization

\section{Related Work}

\paragraph{Multi-turn jailbreak attacks.}
All multi-turn jailbreak families share a persuasion substrate~\citep{zeng2024pap,hagendorff2026reasoning,li2024mhj}, yet each operationalizes it differently: Crescendo~\citep{russinovich2024crescendo} gradually escalates, FITD~\citep{fitd2024} exploits commitment-consistency bias, DD~\citep{deceptivedelight2024} combines emotional manipulation with role assignment, ActorAttack~\citep{ren2024actorattack} steers through multiple personas, and automated methods (PAIR~\citep{chao2023pair}, TAP~\citep{mehrotra2024tap}) iteratively refine adversarial prompts, extending the automated red-teaming paradigm~\citep{perez2022redteaming}. \citet{wei2023jailbroken} provide a unifying taxonomy; RED QUEEN~\citep{jiang2024redqueen} constructs concealed multi-turn scenarios across 14 harmful categories; benchmarks~\citep{mazeika2024harmbench,chao2024jailbreakbench} standardize single-turn comparison, while MultiBreak~\citep{song2026multibreak} targets multi-turn evaluation. This shared substrate with diverse surface strategies defines our evaluation: a detector must generalize across families, not merely succeed on its training distribution.

\paragraph{Multi-turn defenses and production safety.}
No existing defense evaluates cross-attack transfer. Recent conversation-level systems pair fine-tuned BERT~\citep{devlin2019bert} encoders with sequence-level aggregation via GRU hierarchies (DeepContext~\citep{deepcontext2026}), turn-intention inference (BIID~\citep{tong2025biid}), graph neural networks (G-Guard~\citep{gguard2025}), or predictive generation (SafeDream~\citep{safedream2026}), yet each evaluates on a single attack family. Representation-level approaches include X-Boundary~\citep{lu2025xboundary}, JBShield~\citep{zhang2025jbshield} via activated concept analysis, gradient-space analysis (GradSafe~\citep{xie2024gradsafe}), circuit breakers via representation engineering~\citep{zou2024circuitbreakers}, latent activation probing~\citep{kulkarni2026latentadversarial,kadali2026trace}, and vision-language detection~\citep{hua2026rcs}; these require white-box access and can be brittle under adaptive attacks~\citep{derya2026revisitingjbshield}. Our work uses black-box classification with systematic cross-attack OOD evaluation. Per-turn classifiers (Llama Guard~\citep{inan2023llamaguard,metallamaguard3_2024}, ShieldGemma~\citep{zeng2024shieldgemma}, WildGuard~\citep{han2024wildguard}, Constitutional Classifiers~\citep{anthropic2025constitutional}) cannot model multi-turn trajectories, and perturbation-based defenses~\citep{robey2024smoothllm} and safety-aware decoding~\citep{xu2024safedecoding} target single-turn token-level attacks. Our ablation fills this gap: decomposition across thirteen configurations under OOD evaluation identifies domain exposure as the dominant factor behind cross-attack transfer.

\paragraph{Computational persuasion and discourse.}
Persuasion and discourse frameworks offer interpretable vocabulary for attack strategies, but whether such structure aids detection has not been empirically disentangled. Foundational work (RST~\citep{mann1988rst}, PDTB~\citep{prasad2008pdtb}, dialogue acts~\citep{stolcke2000dialogueact}, Speech Act Theory~\citep{austin1962speechacts,searle1969speechacts}) models pragmatic structure at different granularities. Building on Cialdini's principles~\citep{cialdini2001influence}, PAP~\citep{zeng2024pap} identifies 40 persuasion techniques across jailbreak families, and \citet{hagendorff2026reasoning} find identifiable strategies in 97\% of successful attacks; propaganda analysis~\citep{dasanmartino2019fine,dasanmartino2020semeval} and persuasive dialogue~\citep{wang2019persuasion} apply similar technique-level classification. Our ablation disentangles these roles: the taxonomy enables interpretable strategy characterization, but detection operates through domain-adapted embeddings regardless of label correctness.

\paragraph{Domain-adaptive pretraining.}
Continued pretraining on unlabeled domain text yields consistent downstream gains~\citep{gururangan2020dontstop}, extending the pretrain-then-fine-tune approach of ULMFiT~\citep{howard2018ulmfit}, yet this mechanism has not been disentangled for safety-critical generalization. Intermediate-task transfer (STILTs~\citep{phang2018stilts}) and multi-task learning~\citep{caruana1997multitask,liu2019mtdnn} offer complementary paths but confound domain exposure with supervised signal. Our MLM control removes all classification labels and confirms that pretraining alone closes the OOD gap. Dataset artifact analysis~\citep{gururangan2018artifacts,niven2019probing} motivates our TF-IDF baseline, adversarial NLP methods~\citep{jin2020textfooler,li2020bertattack,nasr2025attackermovessecond,andriushchenko2024adaptive} inform the perturbation protocol, and probing techniques~\citep{conneau2018probing,hewitt2019structural} underpin the CKA analysis.

